{
  "requirement": "`habeas_sdk::graph` defines the graph's associated types and content lookup\noperation:\n\n```rust,ignore\npub trait Graph: Send + Sync {\n    /// The *identifier* identifies a particular node in the graph.\n    type NodeId: Clone + Eq + Send + Sync;\n\n    /// The *contents* of a node defines what is given to a validator\n    /// when the node is read.\n    type NodeContent: Send + Sync;\n\n    /// The *label* between edges defines the kind of verification relationship\n    /// between two nodes.\n    type EdgeLabel: Clone + Send + Sync;\n\n    /// User-defined errors that can occur when fetching node content or performing\n    /// other operations.\n    type Error: Send;\n\n    /// Fetch the content of a node.\n    fn node_content(&self, node: &Self::NodeId) -> Result<Self::NodeContent, Self::Error>;\n\n    // ... additional methods defined below ...\n}\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "`requirements.sdk.graph.domain_types` is a type-level contract with one behavioral clause. `Graph` has four associated types, with bounds `NodeId: Clone + Eq + Send + Sync`, `NodeContent: Send + Sync`, `EdgeLabel: Clone + Send + Sync` and `Error: Send`. It also has `node_content(&self, &NodeId) -> Result<NodeContent, Error>`.\n\n**Associated types and bounds.** The compiler enforces these. Several test fixtures implement `Graph` with different concrete types, and the code would not compile if the trait lacked the required associated types or signature:\n- `Memory` in `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/agent.rs` uses `Node`, `Contents`, `Label` and `io::Error`.\n- `Memory` in `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/validation.rs` uses `u8`, `String`, `u8` and `&'static str`.\n- `Application` in `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/contracts.rs` uses `Node`, `Content`, `u8` and `&'static str`.\n- `StoredGraph` in `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/storage.rs` uses `String`, `()`, `()` and `FileStorageError`.\n- `HabeasDomain` in the main crate also implements the trait.\n\nThe marked tests run these implementations through generic SDK code, which needs the bounds to hold. Examples are `Validator`, `ValidationContext` and `InspectionContext`, which the bounds let be shared and sent across threads, as the tokio tests do.\n\n**`node_content` returns the content.**\n- `default_tool_accepts_scalar_results_and_keeps_the_exact_prefix` reads the node through `contents_of_current_node` and checks that the content (\"source\") reaches the agent history and the dependency fingerprint.\n- `review_graph_delegates_unprepared_sources_and_paths_to_the_domain` asserts that `inspection.read(&source)` equals `to_model_input(&graph.domain().node_content(&source).unwrap())`. It also checks that the delivered text contains `call();`.\n- `requirement_edges_and_input_freshness_use_the_sdk` checks that `node_content` returns the fresh content: the fingerprint of the new content differs from the retained one.\n\n**Error result.** The `review_graph_...` test asserts that reading a missing outline item (`fn[missing]`) returns `Err` and records no extra dependency. The fixtures' `Error` types are used as the result error (for example `fail_read` in `contracts.rs` and `validation.rs`), so the `Result<_, Error>` shape is exercised.\n\n**Remaining tests.** `deterministic_validation_uses_required_storage_without_an_agent` and `criteria_keep_typed_answers_and_persist_only_when_requested` use the trait end to end with typed `NodeId` and `NodeContent` values. `habeas_paths_preserve_opaque_segments_and_separate_all_node_kinds` uses `NodeId = HabeasNode`, which compares and clones as `NodeId` requires.\n\nI found no untested obligation or boundary the text names, so I report no gap.",
  "references": "e4961fd64b64b391fe507c475512e66f9de40d4ccc6a13eb41a4d92a577a9c9a",
